          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How do I know whether a three-character category can stand on its own as a usable code?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** The sources I searched don't give a general test for whether a three-character category is complete. They show the pattern and a few examples, but not a full rule. The main points are below.\n\n**What the sources say:**\n\n- **Structure.** An ICD-10-CM code is three to seven characters. The three-character form is the category. Characters four and five add detail such as cause, site, or severity. Character six often records laterality (right, left, unspecified). A seventh character, where a category uses one, describes the episode of care, such as initial treatment, subsequent treatment, or sequela. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n- **Most categories need more characters.** The hypertension guidance says most ICD-10-CM categories need a fourth, fifth, or sixth character before they are billable. I10 (essential hypertension) is an exception and is complete at three characters. (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*.)\n- **Some categories are never used alone.** Asthma (J45) is never used at three characters. Its remaining characters record severity and the state of the disease at the encounter. (Source: *Coding asthma: J45 and its severity and exacerbation axes*.)\n- **The fourth character can be the key.** For COPD (J44), the fourth character records whether a complication is present. The unspecified code is used for a stable presentation. (Source: *Coding chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: J44 and its two complication codes*.)\n\n**What I could not confirm:** The retrieved passages do not list which three-character categories are complete, and they do not give a rule you can apply to any category. For that, check the official ICD-10-CM tabular list, which is the authoritative source for whether a code is valid as written. If you tell me which category you are looking at, I can search for the specific guidance on it.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
